---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const title = 'What Size Battery Does My Car Need?';
const description = 'Car battery sizing — match the group size (H6/H7/BCI), terminal orientation and CCA to the original specification. The fit comes first, then the rating.';
const canonical = '/lead-acid/battery-size-selection/';

const articleJsonLd = {
  '@type': 'Article',
  '@id': `https://worldbatteryhub.com${canonical}#article`,
  headline: title,
  url: `https://worldbatteryhub.com${canonical}`,
  inLanguage: 'en',
  about: 'Car battery size selection',
  articleSection: 'Lead-Acid Battery Knowledge',
  dateModified: '2026-09-16',
  isPartOf: { '@id': 'https://worldbatteryhub.com/#website' },
};

const faqJsonLd = {
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: [
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: 'How do I know what size battery my car needs?',
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: 'Match the group size — the code (H6, H7, BCI 24, etc.) that defines the case dimensions and terminal layout — to the original battery or the owner\u2019s manual. Then match or exceed the CCA rating. The fit comes first; the rating second.',
      },
    },
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: 'Can I put a bigger battery in my car?',
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: 'Only if it physically fits the tray, the terminals match, and the hold-down works. A bigger group with more capacity is fine when it fits; a physically larger battery that does not fit is not.',
      },
    },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonical={canonical}
  ogType="article"
  jsonLd={[articleJsonLd, faqJsonLd]}
  author="World Battery Hub"
  dateModified="2026-09-16"
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="byline">Lead-Acid Knowledge · Practical · Last reviewed 2026-09-16</p>
      <h1>What Size Battery Does My Car Need?</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Car batteries are not one-size-fits-all — the tray, the cables and the clamp were built for a specific
        group, and the right battery is the one that fits that envelope.
      </p>

      <div class="takeaways">
        <strong>Direct answer:</strong> The battery size is the <strong>group code</strong> — H6, H7, BCI 24 and so
        on — which defines the <strong>case dimensions and terminal layout</strong>. Find your car's group (owner's
        manual, the old battery's label, or a fitment lookup), then match or exceed the <strong>CCA</strong>. The
        fit comes first; the rating second.
      </div>

      <h2>The three checks</h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Check</th><th>What to match</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>Group size</strong></td><td>Case dimensions + terminal positions (H6/H7/BCI…)</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>CCA</strong></td><td>Match or exceed the original spec (see <a href="/lead-acid/cca/what-is-cca/">CCA</a>)</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Technology</strong></td><td>Flooded / EFB / AGM as the car requires (start-stop cars need EFB/AGM)</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2>Why the fit comes first</h2>
      <p>
        The tray, the hold-down clamp and the cable reach were all designed for one case shape
        (see <a href="/lead-acid/battery-group-sizes/">battery group sizes</a>). A battery that does not fit cannot
        be secured, and an unsecured battery vibrates, shortens and leaks. Everything else — capacity, brand,
        warranty — is secondary to the case that fits the car.
      </p>

      <h2>Can you go bigger?</h2>
      <p>
        Yes, when it fits: a larger group that sits in the tray with matching terminals and a working hold-down is
        an upgrade, usually with more capacity and CCA. The rule is physical — <strong>if it fits and matches, it
        works</strong>; the rating then only needs to meet or beat the original.
      </p>

      <h2>The Author's Take</h2>
      <div class="takeaways">
        <p>
          <strong>Position:</strong> Battery sizing is a fit problem disguised as a power problem — the group code
          decides almost everything, and the people who start with CCA are answering the second question before
          the first.</p>
        <p>
          <strong>Reasoning:</strong> The tray and terminals are fixed; the rating is flexible upward. Identify the
          group first and the purchase almost decides itself — which is why the fitment lookup, not the spec sheet,
          is the correct starting point.</p>
        <p class="muted"><em>This is the author's editorial view, not a purchasing guarantee.</em></p>
      </div>

      <h2>Sources</h2>
      <p>Cross-verified from BCI group-size references and fitment documentation.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Battery Council International (BCI) — group-size standard.</li>
        <li>Manufacturer fitment documentation — group and CCA requirements.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="muted">Last reviewed: 2026-09-16</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
